<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.7.1: Determining if Zero Is a Solution</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00168</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.7.1: Determining if Zero Is a Solution</md:title>
    <md:uuid>09a26b8e-6de8-446b-ab20-988ca6f322df</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up Activity </title>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Is 0 a <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">solution of an equation</term> \( 4(x + 2) = 10 \)? Explain.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00003">No. For example: If \( x=0 \), then the expression on the left has the value 8, which makes the equation false.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00004">Is 0 a solution for the equation \( 12 - 8x = 3(x + 4) \)? Explain.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00006">Yes. For example: Substituting 0 for \( x \) gives \( 12=12 \), which is true.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00007">Is 0 a solution for the equation \( 5x = \frac12 x \)? Explain.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00009">Yes. For example: 5 multiplied by 0 is 0, and \( \frac{1}{2} \) multiplied by 0 is also 0, so the two sides of the equation are equal.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00010">Is 0 a solution for the equation \( \frac {6}{x} + 1 = 8 \)? Explain.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00012">No. For example: Dividing by 0 gives an undefined result, so it cannot be added by 1 to equal 8.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>